lem with that  would be if agreement isn't reached then  consumer confidence might dip a bit but  in terms of directly affecting us I  think we're good relationships with our  customers and I see that continuing into  the future as well like many other post  industrial towns Dudley voted  overwhelmingly to leave and brexit is  likely to be the elephant in the room  next week the Chancellor unable to make  major decisions until we know what kind  of a deal we're going to get but he  might have a bit more room for giveaways  than normal  here's the intriguing thing over the  past six months the amount of money  coming into the Exchequer has been far  more than anyone expected which raises  two intriguing prospects first of all  might the Chancellor be able to do all  those things he wants to do ending  austerity putting more money into the  NHS without having to raise new taxes  and secondly might the economy actually  be growing a bit faster than everyone  thinks it is in next week's budget the  Chancellor may be able to tell a  cautiously positive story about the  economy as brexit looms large can he  help give hope to the towns which have  been left behind ed conway Sky News in  Dudley
 

